<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The hierarchy kept literal</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-splice-hierarchy">Ten, then one hundred</h2>
    <p class="lede">This assessment does not replace decimal splices with an arbitrary base. TTI’s existing
      Skeletal Splice argument treats base ten as the finding to be defended, not an unnoticed coordinate
      choice.</p>
    <p>The active proposal is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \pi\text{-level geometry}
      \longrightarrow
      10:\text{ localised gravitational source}
      \longrightarrow
      100:\text{ circular phase and electromagnetism}.
    \]</p>
    <p>At the first splice, a deposit or scalar degree of freedom \(\chi\) is proposed to contribute
      stress&ndash;energy while the metric \(g_{\mu\nu}\), not \(\chi\), carries the observed massless spin-two
      mode. At the second splice, a normalised complex degree of freedom
      \(\Psi=R e^{i\theta}\) has phase space \(S^1\simeq U(1)\); local phase comparison motivates a
      connection \(A_\mu\) and covariant derivative \(D_\mu=\nabla_\mu+iqA_\mu\). Matter below the second
      threshold could then gravitate without carrying that electromagnetic phase, suggesting a dark sector.</p>
    <p>That architecture is conditional field theory. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span> It is more disciplined
      than saying “ten is gravity and one hundred is light”, because it names the fields and symmetries that
      would have to exist. It still leaves four independent debts: select one action, introduce a physical
      scale, fix gauge and charge normalisation, and derive a universal radial anchor. The question here is
      whether the RH work pays any of them.</p>
  </div>
</section>

