<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Gate one</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-action-map">No unique splice-to-action map</h2>
    <p>The splice supplies an exact dimensionless sequence. With \(R_n=F_{n+2}-1\), the deposited quantity</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \delta\Pi_n=(F_{n+2}-1)\varphi^{-n}
      =\frac{\varphi^2}{\sqrt5}-\varphi^{-n}
      -\frac{(-1)^n}{\sqrt5}\varphi^{-2n-2}
    \]</p>
    <p>approaches \(L=\varphi^2/\sqrt5\). That limit and its finite corrections are earned arithmetic. They
      do not choose whether \(\delta\Pi_n\) becomes a field value, a source, a coupling, a spectral weight, a
      boundary condition or a non-local kernel.</p>
    <p>The RH archive explores exactly this kind of promotion in a different setting. One can encode data in a
      Hankel matrix, a Loewner matrix, a Jacobi operator, a Weil form or a screw function. Each representation
      exposes a different positivity problem. None is selected merely because the same numbers can be inserted.
      In several cases the desired sign is equivalent to the original unsolved question.</p>
    <p>A splice action therefore needs a declared chain</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \{\delta\Pi_n\}
      \xrightarrow{\;M_1\;}
      \mathcal K(x,y)
      \xrightarrow{\;M_2\;}
      S[g,\chi,\Psi,A],
    \]</p>
    <p>with \(M_1\) and \(M_2\) derived rather than chosen after a desired force law is known. The action must
      be diffeomorphism invariant in its gravitational sector, gauge invariant in its electromagnetic sector,
      bounded or otherwise dynamically stable, and compatible with a conserved total stress tensor. The RH
      archive does not contain those maps. “Cooperative rescue” may suggest checking whether individually
      indefinite terms form a stable global quadratic action, but resemblance is not selection.</p>
    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> RH contributes a warning—positivity of one representation may restate the
      problem—but no unique action. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Gate two</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-gravity-scale">No physical scale for \(G\)</h2>
    <p>Newton’s constant is dimensionful. A sequence of pure numbers, a zeta ordinate, a prime logarithm or a
      positive matrix cannot determine it until a physical unit enters. In a first-splice effective theory one
      may write schematically</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      S_{10}=\int d^4x\sqrt{-g}\left[
        \frac{c^3}{16\pi G}R
        -\frac12(\nabla\chi)^2-V_{10}(\chi)
      \right].
    \]</p>
    <p>Dimensional analysis then permits an expression such as</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      G=C_{10}\frac{c^3\ell_{10}^2}{\hbar}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The splice arithmetic might eventually determine a dimensionless \(C_{10}\), but it cannot produce the
      length \(\ell_{10}\) from no physical input. Nor does the RH zero spectrum help merely by containing
      numbers with a natural order: its ordinates are dimensionless in the standard normalisation. Assigning
      metres, seconds or joules to one of them is a new physical postulate.</p>
    <p>The archive’s shrinking margins sharpen this point. A small dimensionless eigenvalue is not a small
      mass, cosmological constant or coupling until an action fixes how the eigenvalue enters a propagator and
      a scale converts it. Numerical proximity to \(G\), the Planck scale or a galactic scale after unit choices
      would be fitting, not derivation.</p>
    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> no physical scale appears. The open requirement for \(\ell_{10}\) survives
      unchanged. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Gate three</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-charge-normalisation">No unique \(e\) or \(\alpha_{\rm EM}\)</h2>
    <p>A second-splice phase can motivate the topology of electromagnetism. If \(\Psi=R e^{i\theta}\) and
      phase comparison is local, then</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      D_\mu\Psi=(\nabla_\mu+iqA_\mu)\Psi,
      \qquad
      F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu.
    \]</p>
    <p>Exact unbroken local \(U(1)\) would forbid a bare Proca mass, and an anomaly-free current could be
      conserved. Opposite winding orientations can represent opposite signs of charge. These are conditional
      structural consequences, not yet the observed charge spectrum.</p>
    <p>The normalisation freedom remains:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      A_\mu\mapsto\lambda A_\mu,
      \qquad q\mapsto\frac{q}{\lambda}.
    \]</p>
    <p>Only a canonically normalised kinetic term and an independently selected smallest representation fix
      what number is called \(e\). Predicting \(\alpha_{\rm EM}=e^2/(4\pi\hbar c)\) also requires the scale at
      which the boundary value is set and the renormalisation-group flow to the measured scale.</p>
    <p>The RH archive contains circle symmetries, transforms and spectral operators, but no theorem selecting a
      unique gauge kinetic coefficient or charge lattice for the splice. A zeta regularisation may assign a
      finite value to an expression once the physical operator is known; it cannot choose the operator or its
      field normalisation in advance.</p>
    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> the route from second-splice phase to \(U(1)\) remains plausible as a symmetry
      promotion, while the numerical coupling remains underdetermined. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Gate four</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-halo-radius">No independent \(r_0\) for the halo prediction</h2>
    <p>The most concrete splice consequence found so far begins by mapping levels to decimal radii,</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      r_n=r_0\,10^n,
      \qquad
      \Delta\Phi_n=\kappa_{10}\delta\Pi_n.
    \]</p>
    <p>If the deposit per decade approaches \(L\), then</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \Phi(r)\longrightarrow\frac{\kappa_{10}L}{\ln10}\ln\!\left(\frac r{r_0}\right),
      \qquad
      v_c^2\longrightarrow\frac{\kappa_{10}L}{\ln10},
      \qquad
      \rho(r)=\frac{v_\infty^2}{4\pi G r^2}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The finite corrections predict an approach exponent
      \(p=\ln\varphi/\ln10\approx0.20899\) and a decaying decimal log-periodic residual. That is an
      interesting conditional signature. It becomes predictive only if \(r_0\) is fixed independently and the
      same source explains both dynamics and lensing.</p>
    <p>The RH archive contains logarithmic prime frequencies and zero ordinates, so it is tempting to identify
      one with \(r_0\). No unique dimensionally valid map does so. Choosing the first zero, a mean spacing or a
      prime scale because it improves a galaxy fit would introduce exactly the retrospective freedom the
      prediction is meant to remove. One universal anchor must be derived before looking at individual rotation
      curves, or tied to a separately measured formation variable by one preregistered law.</p>
    <p><strong>Answer:</strong> no independent radius emerges from RH. The conditional flat-curve and
      log-periodic results remain intact, but not calibrated. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

