<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">What can be carried across</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-splice-tests">Four RH-inspired tests worth doing</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>1. Transform before interpreting</h3>
      <p>The exact splice generating function</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        \mathcal P(z)=\sum_{n\ge1}\delta\Pi_nz^n
        =\frac{x(2+x)}{1-x-x^2}-\frac{x}{1-x},
        \qquad x=\frac z\varphi,
      \]</p>
      <p>makes poles, convergence and asymptotics visible. The next step is to define which transform is meant
        to become a spacetime kernel and why that transform, not a convenient alternative, preserves the
        decimal-splice interpretation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>2. Certify positivity with an error budget</h3>
      <p>If stability depends on a Hessian, Hankel matrix or quadratic kernel, state the infinite object first.
        Derive a truncation bound, then classify each finite computation as positive, negative or inconclusive.
        A tiny eigenvalue inside an omitted-tail budget is not evidence of rescue.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>3. Preregister the decimal residual</h3>
      <p>The literal-base prediction has log-frequency \(2\pi/\ln10\) and golden approach exponent
        \(\ln\varphi/\ln10\). Fix amplitude law, phase rule, radial anchor and covariance before examining a
        galaxy sample. Do not permit a separate phase and \(r_0\) for every object.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>4. Demand one source for dynamics and lensing</h3>
      <p>If first-splice matter gravitates, its stress tensor must curve the same metric seen by massive
        tracers and light. A rotation-curve fit without the corresponding lensing, growth and CMB effects is
        not a dark-matter theory. This requirement comes from gravity, not RH, but RH’s identity-gap lesson
        reinforces the refusal to infer a global mechanism from one projection.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">A possible new falsification programme</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-decimal-spectrum-test">The decimal-spectrum test</h2>
    <p>One genuinely useful synthesis is methodological. The splice predicts discrete scale structure; the RH
      work is expert at turning arithmetic sequences into spectra and at exposing how finite transforms can
      mislead. TTI can therefore formulate a preregistered decimal-spectrum test without claiming that zeta
      zeros cause galaxies.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Derive the residual shape from the exact \(\delta\Pi_n\), including both the monotone
        \(\varphi^{-n}\) term and the alternating \((-1)^n\varphi^{-2n-2}\) term.</li>
      <li>Map \(n=\log_{10}(r/r_0)\) with one independently specified rule for \(r_0\).</li>
      <li>Construct the predicted covariance in \(\log r\), including measurement windows and baryonic-model
        uncertainty.</li>
      <li>Fit a conventional smooth-halo null and the preregistered splice alternative to rotation and lensing
        data jointly.</li>
      <li>Penalise every free phase, anchor or object-specific amplitude. Reject the splice residual if its
        out-of-sample likelihood does not beat the null by the declared threshold.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>RH contributes no physical coefficient to this protocol. It contributes the insistence that a spectral
      pattern needs a convergence domain, an uncertainty budget and a universal quantifier stated honestly.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">Final answer</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-splice-verdict">Helpful, but not in the way a shortcut would be</h2>
    <p>The consolidated RH work does not solve any of the four outstanding splice derivations. It supplies no
      unique action coefficient, no unit-bearing scale, no electromagnetic normalisation and no radius. It
      also does not undermine the literal 10-to-100 hierarchy merely because one RH search found no Fibonacci
      connection to its selected matrices.</p>
    <p>What it gives TTI is a better laboratory discipline: propagate corrections, distinguish identities from
      signs, distinguish signs from mechanisms, and make truncation error smaller than the margin before naming
      a finite pattern universal. That is real progress. The theory’s next advance must still come from a
      splice-to-action map that earns its units and observables.</p>
